HTTP Parameter Pollution is a type of web application vulnerability that occurs when an application does not properly validate and sanitize user input. This can allow an attacker to bypass the application's input validation and WAF rules by sending the same input in multiple HTTP request parameters.
Here is an example of how to exploit HTTP Parameter Pollution in a PHP application:
```
<?php
if (isset($_GET['name'])) {
    $name = $_GET['name'];
} elseif (isset($_POST['name'])) {
    $name = $_POST['name'];
} else {
    $name = 'default';
}
echo '<h1>Hello, '. $name. '!</h1>';
?>
```
In this example, the application checks if the name parameter exists in the GET or POST request. If it exists in the GET request, the application will use its value. If it exists in the POST request, the application will use its value. If it does not exist in either request, the application will use the value "default".
An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by sending the same input in multiple HTTP request parameters. For example, the attacker can send the input value in both the GET and POST requests. The application will then use the value from the GET request, even though the application checks if the value exists in the POST request.
